title: Work-up of prostate cancer recurrence after radiation
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Key decision:'' Establish PSA kinetics and disease location before choosing [[local salvage|Local salvage for prostate cancer recurrence after radiation]] versus [[monitoring/systemic treatment|Monitoring and systemic treatment of prostate cancer recurrence after radiation]].
* ''Definition:'' After definitive radiation, biochemical recurrence is PSA ≥2 ng/mL above the post-treatment nadir (Phoenix), including after radiation plus androgen deprivation therapy (ADT). PSA need not become undetectable; do not apply the post-prostatectomy 0.2-ng/mL threshold.
* ''Confirm:'' Repeat PSA to establish a sustained rise; consider benign PSA bounce, inflammation, and testosterone recovery after ADT. Review nadir, PSA doubling time (PSADT), interval since radiation, original Grade Group, prior radiation fields, ADT exposure, symptoms, and digital rectal examination. Check testosterone when prior/ongoing ADT affects interpretation.
* ''Do not wait mechanically:'' A confirmed rapid rise or suspicious examination warrants evaluation before Phoenix criteria, particularly in fit local-salvage candidates.
* ''Localize:'' Obtain PSMA (prostate-specific membrane antigen) PET/CT for restaging when results change management; multiparametric prostate MRI maps local disease and guides biopsy. Negative imaging does not exclude microscopic cancer; investigate atypical PET findings before changing treatment intent.
* ''Before local salvage:'' Require biopsy confirmation of viable recurrence, with targeted sampling of suspicious areas and seminal vesicles as appropriate; PET uptake alone is insufficient. Radiation effects can complicate early biopsy interpretation.

Sources: NCCN Prostate Cancer v7.2026, PROS-10/10A, MS-36; ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 14, pp. 754, 757–758 (PDF 761, 764–765); [[AUA/ASTRO/SUO 2024|https://www.auanet.org/documents/Guidelines/PDF/2024%20Guidelines/STPC%20Unabridged%20Final.pdf]], statement 23.
